{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>You shall not take the name of YHWH your Elohim in vain.</b> This verse has already been explained in the words of our Sages (Shevuot 21a) that it prohibits swearing by the honored Name for naught, such as one who swears about something known to a person — to alter it or to affirm it — about a pillar of marble that it is of gold, or that it is of marble, when it stands before them and they recognize it. And according to the plain sense of Scripture, it would further prohibit that one not bear upon his lips the honored Name for nothing, as in the language of <b>לֹא תִשָּׂא שֵׁמַע שָׁוְא</b> (Exodus 23:1) ('You shall not bear a false report'), and <b>וּבַל אֶשָּׂא אֶת שְׁמוֹתָם עַל שְׂפָתָי</b> (Psalm 16:4) ('nor shall I bear their names upon my lips'), for speech is called thus (נשיאה) on account of lifting up one's voice in it. And similarly <b>מַשָּׂא דְּבַר ה'</b> (Zechariah 9:1) ('The burden of the word of YHWH'), and similarly <b>יִשָּׂא בַיּוֹם הַהוּא לֵאמֹר לֹא אֶהְיֶה חֹבֵשׁ</b> (Isaiah 3:7) ('he will lift up his voice on that day, saying, I shall not be a healer') — he will lift up his voice to say thus.<br>And in truth this too is forbidden, and in the language of the Sages it is called מוציא שם שמים לבטלה (uttering the Name of Heaven for nothing) (Temurah 3b). And our Sages already said (Sifra Leviticus ch. 2; Nedarim 10a): From where do we learn that a person should not say 'la-YHWH an olah,' 'la-YHWH a sin-offering,' but rather should say 'an olah la-YHWH,' 'a sin-offering la-YHWH'? Scripture says, <b>קָרְבָּן לַה'</b> (Leviticus 1:2) ('A sacrifice to YHWH') — and is this not an argument a fortiori: if regarding one who is about to consecrate, the Torah said that My Name should not apply except to the sacrifice, then a fortiori, etc. And this commandment was arranged after the warning against idolatry, for just as it is fitting to fear YHWH the great and awesome, not to give His honor to another, so too it is fitting to give honor to His Name, and one who bears it in vain profanes it, as in the matter that is written: <b>וְלֹא תִשָּׁבְעוּ בִשְׁמִי לַשָּׁקֶר וְחִלַּלְתָּ אֶת שֵׁם אֱלֹהֶיךָ</b> (Leviticus 19:12) ('And you shall not swear by My Name falsely, and you shall not profane the name of your Elohim'). And just as He was stringent regarding idolatry and wrote the punishment, 'for He is a jealous El, visiting the iniquity of the fathers upon the children,' so too He wrote in this case the punishment, 'for He will not acquit him.' And He spoke in this language and did not say 'for He will punish him for it,' because in the eyes of the one who swears it does not appear a complete transgression and he thinks it is fitting to forgive him; therefore He said that He will not acquit anyone who touches it. And the words of R. Abraham ibn Ezra on this verse are proper.<br>Now the language of this verse is <b>אֶת שֵׁם ה' אֱלֹהֶיךָ</b> ('the name of YHWH your Elohim'), as though Moses were speaking, and so in all the commandments thereafter. But in the first two verses the blessed One speaks: <b>אָנֹכִי</b>, <b>אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתִיךָ</b>, <b>עַל פָּנָי</b>, <b>כִּי אָנֹכִי</b>, <b>לְאֹהֲבַי וּלְשׁוֹמְרֵי מִצְוֹתָי</b> — and for this reason our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Makkot 24a): 'I am' and 'You shall have no other' — from the mouth of the Almighty we heard them, for they are the foundation of everything. And ibn Ezra raised the difficulty, for Scripture says (Exodus 20:1), <b>וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים אֵת כָּל הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה</b> ('And Elohim spoke all these words'), and it is explicit from this: <b>אֶת הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה דִּבֶּר ה' אֶל כָּל קְהַלְכֶם</b> (Deuteronomy 5:19) ('These words YHWH spoke to all your assembly'), and there it is further written <b>וַיִּכְתְּבֵם עַל שְׁנֵי לֻחֹת אֲבָנִים</b> ('and He wrote them on two tablets of stone') — for just as He spoke them to all your assembly, so He wrote them on the tablets. But I shall explain to you the tradition of our Sages. Surely all ten commandments were heard by all Israel from the mouth of Elohim according to the plain sense of Scripture. But in the first two commandments, they would hear the utterance and understand it from Him (YHWH) Himself, just as they would understand it from Moses; and therefore He would speak with them as a master speaks to his servant, as I have mentioned. And from that point onward, in the rest of the commandments, they would hear the sound of the utterance but would not understand it, and Moses would need to translate every single utterance for them until they understood it from Moses himself. And thus they explain <b>מֹשֶׁה יְדַבֵּר וְהָאֱלֹהִים יַעֲנֶנּוּ בְקוֹל</b> (Exodus 19:19) ('Moses will speak and HaElohim will answer him with a voice'). And therefore the words of YHWH were interwoven with Moses, that he would say them to the people. And the intention in this was so that they would all become prophets in the faith of YHWH and in the prohibition of idolatry, as I explained (ad loc. ch. 9), because these are the foundation of the entire Torah and the commandments, as He said: <b>הַקְהֶל לִי אֶת הָעָם וְאַשְׁמִעֵם אֶת דְּבָרָי אֲשֶׁר יִלְמְדוּן לְיִרְאָה אֹתִי כָּל הַיָּמִים</b> (Deuteronomy 4:10) ('Assemble the people for Me and I will make them hear My words, by which they shall learn to fear Me all the days'). But in the rest of the commandments, they would receive from the mouth of Moses in their explanation together with their hearing the sound of the words, and in the rest of the commandments they would believe in Moses with everything."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :register {:flags {0 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:11:53.374136735Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}